I'm setting up a four-level role hierarchy with Admin at the top for system-wide technical management, followed by Pemilik as the organizational leader, then Atasan and Pengguna below. The key distinction is that Admin handles user and role management plus audit logs but stays outside the organizational hierarchy—they don't participate in content consumption, read receipts, training stats, or exams meant for employees.

For Pemilik, they oversee the entire organization across all units and can manage Atasan assignments while viewing organization-wide statistics. Atasan (unit heads) manage only their own staff, create announcements and trainings scoped to their unit, and see stats limited to their bidang. Pengguna are staff who consume content assigned by their Atasan and organization-wide announcements from leadership.

I'm defining visibility rules where announcements and trainings created by Atasan target only their bidang audience, while Pemilik creates organization-wide content visible to everyone. Admin can create system notices but shouldn't be counted in reader statistics since they're not part of the employee tracking system.

Now I'm planning the implementation: updating the role enum to add 'pemilik', adding a unit_kerja scope field to announcements and trainings (nullable for org-wide), and modifying controllers so Atasan automatically scope content to their own unit while Pemilik/Admin can choose specific bidang or organization-wide. I'll also need to filter visibility queries and exclude admin from completion percentage calculations. ete the implementation plan. For the migration, I'll modify the users table to add 'pemilik' to the role enum using a raw SQL statement to avoid needing the doctrine/dbal package. Then I'll add unit_kerja columns to both announcements and trainings tables to scope visibility by bidang, with null representing org-wide access.

Next, I need to update the model methods so readPercentage() and completionPercentage() only count non-admin users within the appropriate scope. Then in the controllers, I'll filter what announcements each user sees based on their unit_kerja, while atasan users automatically get their own unit assigned when creating announcements, and pemilik/admin users can select any unit from a dropdown.

For TrainingController, I'll apply the same scoping logic. The authorizeManage() check stays the same—pegawai can't create, but atasan/pemilik/admin can. For LetterController, I'll keep letters unscoped organization-wide for simplicity, but the key fix is excluding admins from appearing in read-receipt displays when users open announcements or letters, since that was the main confusion point.

